<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Orange River Sovereignty</h1><p class="st-meta">1848 CE – 1854 CE · capital Bloemfontein</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Britain's brief attempt to rule the land between the Orange and Vaal rivers, abandoned in 1854 in favour of the Orange Free State.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sir-harry-smith-1st-baronet">Sir Harry Smith</a> proclaimed British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the territory between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-orange">Orange</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaal-river">Vaal</a> rivers in February 1848, hoping to control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> and keep peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-pretorius">Andries Pretorius</a> rose against the annexation but was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boomplaats">Boomplaats</a> in August 1848.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A British Resident, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-douglas-warden">Major Henry Warden</a>, governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> with little money and few troops. His boundary lines between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> farms and the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/basotho-kingdom">Basotho Kingdom</a> satisfied no one, and British forces were defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-viervoet">Viervoet</a> in 1851 and fought an indecisive battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-berea">Berea</a> in 1852. Boer farmers resented taxes and the lack of protection.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> decided the territory was not worth its cost. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein-convention">Bloemfontein Convention</a> of 23 February 1854 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> withdrew, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State</a>, which kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> as its capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/orange-river-sovereignty.jpg" alt="Orange River Sovereignty" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Original: Acts of Union 1800 Vector: Yaddah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(3-5).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1848 CE – 1854 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bloemfontein</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sir Harry Smith, Henry Warden</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Christianity, Sotho traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch, Sesotho</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>South Africa</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Orange River Sovereignty (British)</dd></dl></aside></div>
